          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Tekton vs Stanley",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/tekton-vs-stanley/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Tekton better than Stanley?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For a socket and wrench collection built over time, Tekton is generally the stronger choice: a 90-tooth ratchet across its quick-release line, consistent sets built from single components, and an online photo warranty. Stanley is the better buy for a complete mixed kit from a store shelf, often on promotion. Both put lifetime coverage on mechanics tools."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is the Stanley mechanics tool warranty lifetime?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes in the United States. Stanley's warranty page says its mechanics tools, meaning wrenches, sockets, ratchets and drive accessories, carry a full lifetime warranty: if one fails for any reason, Stanley replaces it with no proof of purchase. Claims go through its phone line rather than the store. Canadian product pages describe some sets as limited lifetime."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Where are Stanley mechanics tools made?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Stanley does not publish a country-of-origin breakdown. Retailer listings show individual sets sourced from Taiwan or China, and listings for the same set sometimes disagree. The country is marked on the tool or packaging, which is the reliable check. Tekton publishes its breakdown: about 82 percent Taiwan and 12 percent USA."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are Tekton sockets better than Stanley sockets?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Both use chrome vanadium for hand sockets and both offer 6-point designs, so the steel is comparable. Tekton's advantage is its system: sets built from the same parts it sells singly, no skipped sizes, and easy single replacements. Stanley's sockets usually come inside larger mixed kits with bits and hex keys, which suits household use."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Stanley own Craftsman and DeWalt?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Stanley Black & Decker, Stanley's parent company, owns DeWalt and Craftsman along with Black+Decker, Irwin, Mac Tools, Proto and others. It bought Craftsman from Sears in 2017. Each brand has its own product line, pricing, distribution and warranty terms, so a Stanley tool and a Craftsman tool are not interchangeable for warranty purposes."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which has the better ratchet, Tekton or Stanley?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "On published specifications, Tekton's quick-release ratchets have a 90-tooth gear with a 4-degree swing arc, while Stanley's standard 3/8 inch pear-head quick-release ratchet has 72 teeth. That gives Tekton the finer action in tight spaces. Stanley's premium FATMAX line includes a 120-tooth ratchet, so compare specific models."
          }
        },
