      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Buy Tekton if you are building a socket and wrench collection piece by piece and want the most consistent line for the money. Buy Stanley if you want a complete mixed kit from a store shelf today, at a price that is often discounted. Both brands put a lifetime warranty with no receipt on their mechanics tools, so the choice comes down to range, ratchet specification, how you like to shop, and how much of the box you will actually use.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Who Each Brand Actually Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tekton is a family-owned hand tool company in Grand Rapids, Michigan that sells mainly direct through its own website, with Amazon and a limited number of authorized dealers as secondary channels. It focuses on hand tools and storage: sockets, ratchets, wrenches, screwdrivers, pliers, hex keys, torque wrenches and the organizers that hold them. Its pitch is a single, consistent line of tools, with sets built from the same individual components it sells singly. Our <a href="/tekton-tools-review/">full Tekton tools review</a> covers the range in depth.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stanley is a much older and much broader brand. It is one of the tool brands of Stanley Black & Decker, the Connecticut-based company formed when Stanley merged with Black & Decker in 2010. Under the Stanley name you will find tape measures, levels, utility knives, hammers, storage and power tools, and mechanics tools are only one part of that catalog. Stanley also has a premium sub-line, FATMAX, which its own site describes as its highest-quality range.
          </p>

          <p>
            The parent company matters for this comparison. Stanley Black & Decker's brand list includes DeWalt, Craftsman, Black+Decker, Irwin, Mac Tools, Proto, Facom and others alongside Stanley. Craftsman, the brand many Americans associate with mechanics tools, was bought from Sears in 2017. So when you compare Tekton with Stanley, you are comparing one specialist with one of several mechanics tool brands under the same corporate roof, each with its own price point, distribution and warranty terms.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice, Stanley mechanics tools sit at the value end of that family. They are sold largely as mixed sets through home centres, farm stores and online retailers, often bundled with bits, hex keys and nut drivers in a blow-molded case. Tekton sits in a similar price band but is organized differently: fewer giant mixed kits, more focused sets and individual tools you can add to over time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="made" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Where Tekton and Stanley Tools Are Made
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tekton is unusually open about origin. Its About Us page breaks the current lineup down as roughly 82 percent made in Taiwan, 12 percent in the United States, 3.5 percent in Germany and 2 percent in Canada, with a handful of items from elsewhere in Europe. Some wrenches, picks, hooks and pry bars are made in its own Michigan facilities from U.S.-sourced steel. Every product page lists the country of origin. Our explainer on <a href="/where-are-tekton-tools-made/">where Tekton tools are made</a> walks through how to check any individual item.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stanley does not publish an equivalent breakdown for its mechanics tools. Retailer and distributor listings give country of origin for individual sets, and those listings vary: a Canadian distributor lists the 60-piece STMT71650 mechanics set as made in Taiwan, while listings for the 201-piece STMT71654 set disagree, with one seller showing Taiwan and another China. The reasonable reading is that Stanley mechanics tools are sourced from Asian factories, with origin varying by product and over time.
          </p>

          <p>
            If origin matters to you, check the marking on the tool itself. U.S. customs rules require imported goods to carry a country-of-origin mark, and on hand tools it is usually stamped or etched into the body or printed on the packaging. With Tekton you can also check the product page before buying. With Stanley, the package and the tool are the reliable sources.
          </p>

          <p>
            As we say throughout our Tekton coverage, country of origin is a weak proxy for quality. Taiwan has been a major source of well-regarded hand tools for decades, and what separates two sockets from the same region is the specification the brand orders and how strictly it checks what comes back. The more useful questions are which brand gives you the specification you need and what happens when a tool fails.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Warranties Compared Clause by Clause
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both warranties are good, and closer than most brand comparisons. Stanley's U.S. warranty page states that its mechanics tools, which it defines as wrenches, sockets, ratchets and drive accessories, are covered by a full lifetime warranty: if any Stanley mechanics tool ever fails for any reason, Stanley will replace it, and no proof of purchase is required. The only exclusion listed for that category is incidental or consequential damages.
          </p>

          <p>
            The claim process is by phone. Stanley's page tells owners not to return mechanics tools to the store and instead to call its warranty line, 1-800-262-2161, Monday to Friday, 8 a.m. to 5 p.m. Eastern, after which Stanley ships a replacement. Stanley's other hand tools, outside the mechanics category, carry a separate limited lifetime warranty for as long as the original purchaser owns the product, which excludes excessive wear, misuse, abuse and consumables such as screwdriver bits. Note too that Stanley's Canadian product pages describe some mechanics sets as having a limited lifetime warranty, so terms differ by country.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tekton's warranty is summarized on its own page as no time limits, no receipts, and shipping on Tekton. Claims go through an online form with photos of the damaged tool, ideally with your name and the date in the picture, and Tekton says a complete request received before 2 p.m. Eastern on a weekday can ship the same day. Its FAQ says it covers its tools regardless of where or when they were bought, and replacements ship within the United States and Canada.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Term</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tekton</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Stanley (U.S. mechanics tools)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Duration</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No time limit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Full lifetime</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Proof of purchase</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not required</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not required</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Who is covered</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whoever owns the tool</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not limited on the mechanics tools page</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">How to claim</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Online form with photos</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Phone the warranty line; do not return to store</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Replacement</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Shipped, Tekton pays shipping</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Shipped by Stanley</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Stated exclusions</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None listed; tool must have failed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Incidental or consequential damages</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The practical difference is convenience rather than coverage. Tekton's photo-based online claim with same-day shipping is about as easy as warranties get. Stanley's phone process is perfectly workable but depends on calling during business hours. Neither offers an over-the-counter swap, which is the one thing a store-exchange brand gives you; Craftsman's warranty page, for comparison, tells owners to return products to the original place of purchase for replacement, and Harbor Freight exchanges Icon tools in its stores, as covered in <a href="/tekton-vs-icon/">Tekton vs Icon</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sockets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Tekton vs Stanley Sockets and Ratchets
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ratchets show the clearest specification difference. Tekton's current quick-release ratchets use a 90-tooth gear, which its product pages say gives a 4-degree swing arc, with a single half-moon pawl that keeps nine teeth engaged. Stanley's standard 3/8 inch pear-head quick-release ratchet, model 91-929, has a 72-tooth gear according to Stanley's Canadian product page. More teeth mean a smaller swing to catch the next tooth, which matters in tight engine bays and behind appliances. Our explainer on <a href="/ratchet-tooth-count-explained/">ratchet tooth count</a> covers the trade-off between fine and coarse mechanisms.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stanley does make finer ratchets. Its UK site lists a FATMAX 3/8 inch ratchet with 120 teeth, but that is the premium sub-line and not what you typically get in a mixed mechanics set. When comparing sets, look at the ratchet model inside the case rather than assuming every Stanley or Tekton ratchet shares one specification.
          </p>

          <p>
            On sockets, both brands use the correct basic materials. Retailer listings for Stanley's 201-piece STMT71654 set describe chrome vanadium sockets with a polished chrome finish, 6-point sockets across 1/4, 3/8 and 1/2 inch drives, and a statement that the tools meet or exceed ANSI specifications. Tekton sells 6-point and 12-point sockets in chrome vanadium for hand use and chrome molybdenum for impact use. Our guides to <a href="/6-point-vs-12-point-sockets/">6-point vs 12-point sockets</a> and <a href="/chrome-vanadium-vs-chrome-molybdenum-sockets/">chrome vanadium vs chrome molybdenum</a> explain why those details matter.
          </p>

          <p>
            The structural difference is how the sets are built. Stanley's big mechanics sets are mixed kits. The 201-piece set, for example, includes three ratchets, sockets, 28 hex keys, 12 nut bits and 55 bits, according to Stanley's Canadian listing. That is convenient, but the headline piece count is driven by small bits. Tekton builds its sets from the same components it sells individually and says it avoids skipping sizes, so a lost 10 mm socket is a single replacement part and a set can be completed over time.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are choosing a drive size, our guide to <a href="/what-drive-size-socket-set-do-i-need/">which drive size socket set you need</a> is the place to start. As a rule, a 3/8 inch drive covers most household and automotive work, 1/4 inch handles small fasteners, and 1/2 inch is for suspension, lug nuts and anything seized.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wrenches" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Tekton vs Stanley Wrenches
          </h2>

          <p>
            Combination wrenches are Tekton's strongest category, and the reason it has the following it does. Our <a href="/tekton-vs-gearwrench/">Tekton vs GearWrench comparison</a> covers why: consistent jaw and box-end dimensions across a set matter more than finish, because loose-fitting wrenches round fasteners. Tekton also makes some of its wrenches in its own Michigan facilities, which is relevant if you want American-made tools in the most-used part of the box. Check the origin on each product page, because not every Tekton wrench is U.S.-made.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stanley sells combination wrench sets in SAE and metric, and its UK wrench page describes sets in polished or black chrome finishes. Some listings use Stanley's MaxDrive name for a box-end profile it markets as reducing slippage. Stanley also sells ratcheting combination wrench sets, such as a 20-piece set covering both metric and SAE sizes, according to retailer listings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ratcheting wrenches are worth singling out. Neither brand leads that category; GearWrench does, as our GearWrench comparison explains. If ratcheting wrenches are a big part of your work, it can make sense to buy them from a specialist and fill the rest of the box from Tekton or Stanley.
          </p>

          <p>
            One quirk of the Stanley Black & Decker family is that retailer catalogs sometimes blur the brands. Some industrial distributors list DeWalt-branded mechanics tools, with DWMT part numbers, under the Stanley name. If you are comparing specifications or warranties, confirm which brand is actually printed on the tool, because each brand has its own warranty page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Price, Availability and How You Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both brands sit in the value-to-mid price band, below professional tool-truck brands such as Snap-on, which we compare with Tekton in <a href="/tekton-vs-snap-on/">Tekton vs Snap-on</a>. Within that band, the comparison depends on what you are buying. A large Stanley mixed set often looks cheaper per piece, but much of the count is bits and hex keys. Compare sets by counting the sockets, wrenches and ratchets you will actually use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tekton sells mainly direct, and its own FAQ says it typically does not run sales, so the listed price is close to the price you pay. It also warns that marketplace sellers sometimes list older models, used tools or counterfeits, which is a reason to buy from Tekton's site or a known dealer. Stanley sells through a broad retail network, including home centres, farm and hardware stores and online retailers, where promotional pricing on big sets is common.
          </p>

          <p>
            Availability favours Stanley. You can usually walk into a store and buy a Stanley set today. Tekton is usually an online order and a short wait. For a home mechanic, that rarely matters. For anyone who needs a tool to finish a job this afternoon, it does.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">What the Spec Sheets Say</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">90</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Tekton quick-release ratchet teeth</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">72</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Stanley 91-929 ratchet teeth</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">82%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Tekton lineup made in Taiwan</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Receipts needed by either brand</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Neither brand is the place to buy a torque wrench on price alone. If you need one, our <a href="/best-torque-wrench/">torque wrench guide</a> compares accuracy and calibration options across brands, including Tekton's click-type models.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Who Should Buy Which
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy Tekton if you are building a collection over time and want consistency. Its strengths are combination wrenches, focused socket sets, the 90-tooth gear its product pages list for its quick-release ratchets, sets built from replaceable single components, and a warranty claimed online with photos. It suits a home garage that is steadily turning into a workshop, and anyone who wants a few U.S.-made wrenches in the box. Our <a href="/best-tekton-hand-tools/">best Tekton hand tools</a> guide picks the individual items worth starting with.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy Stanley if you want one case that covers everything for household jobs, bought from a store today, ideally on promotion. A mixed Stanley set with sockets, bits, hex keys and ratchets handles furniture assembly, appliance installs, bike work and the lag bolts on a project like hanging a sliding barn door track. If sockets are the main thing you need, our <a href="/tekton-sockets-review/">Tekton sockets review</a> covers what the dedicated sets add. The full lifetime warranty on its mechanics tools means a broken ratchet is a phone call, not a new purchase.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you like the idea of Stanley's parent company but want a store exchange, Craftsman is the sibling brand sold in-store whose warranty page directs owners back to the original place of purchase. If you want a finer ratchet from Stanley itself, look at FATMAX rather than the standard line.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mixing brands is perfectly reasonable. A common pattern is a Stanley or similar mixed set for the kitchen drawer and odd jobs, plus a Tekton socket set and wrench set in the garage for anything involving a vehicle or seized hardware. Both warranties cover the tools for life, so the choice is less about risk and more about how you like to buy and what you actually use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing sets by piece count</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Big mixed sets inflate their counts with screwdriver bits and hex keys. Count the sockets, ratchets and wrenches you will use, and check that the sizes you need are not skipped, before deciding which set is the better value.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every ratchet in a brand is the same</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tekton's quick-release ratchets list 90 teeth, Stanley's standard 91-929 lists 72, and Stanley's FATMAX line includes a 120-tooth ratchet. Check the model of the ratchet inside the case rather than relying on the brand name.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking a store's word on country of origin</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Retailer listings for the same Stanley set disagree about origin, and older Tekton reviews can be out of date. Read the mark on the tool or packaging, and with Tekton check the specifications line on the product page.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Returning a broken Stanley tool to the store</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stanley's U.S. warranty page says not to return mechanics tools to the retailer. Call the warranty line instead. Tekton claims go through its online form with photos, and neither brand needs a receipt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using chrome hand sockets on an impact driver</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Chrome vanadium hand sockets are not designed for impact tools and can crack. Use chrome molybdenum impact sockets with impact drivers and wrenches, whichever brand you buy, and expect warranties to treat obvious misuse differently from a failure.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Tekton better than Stanley?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a socket and wrench collection built over time, Tekton is generally the stronger choice: a 90-tooth ratchet across its quick-release line, consistent sets built from single components, and an online photo warranty. Stanley is the better buy for a complete mixed kit from a store shelf, often on promotion. Both put lifetime coverage on mechanics tools.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Stanley mechanics tool warranty lifetime?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes in the United States. Stanley's warranty page says its mechanics tools, meaning wrenches, sockets, ratchets and drive accessories, carry a full lifetime warranty: if one fails for any reason, Stanley replaces it with no proof of purchase. Claims go through its phone line rather than the store. Canadian product pages describe some sets as limited lifetime.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where are Stanley mechanics tools made?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Stanley does not publish a country-of-origin breakdown. Retailer listings show individual sets sourced from Taiwan or China, and listings for the same set sometimes disagree. The country is marked on the tool or packaging, which is the reliable check. Tekton publishes its breakdown: about 82 percent Taiwan and 12 percent USA.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Tekton sockets better than Stanley sockets?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both use chrome vanadium for hand sockets and both offer 6-point designs, so the steel is comparable. Tekton's advantage is its system: sets built from the same parts it sells singly, no skipped sizes, and easy single replacements. Stanley's sockets usually come inside larger mixed kits with bits and hex keys, which suits household use.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Stanley own Craftsman and DeWalt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Stanley Black & Decker, Stanley's parent company, owns DeWalt and Craftsman along with Black+Decker, Irwin, Mac Tools, Proto and others. It bought Craftsman from Sears in 2017. Each brand has its own product line, pricing, distribution and warranty terms, so a Stanley tool and a Craftsman tool are not interchangeable for warranty purposes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which has the better ratchet, Tekton or Stanley?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On published specifications, Tekton's quick-release ratchets have a 90-tooth gear with a 4-degree swing arc, while Stanley's standard 3/8 inch pear-head quick-release ratchet has 72 teeth. That gives Tekton the finer action in tight spaces. Stanley's premium FATMAX line includes a 120-tooth ratchet, so compare specific models.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I buy Tekton tools in stores?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mostly not. Tekton sells mainly through its own website, with Amazon and a limited number of authorized dealers as other channels, and it warns that some marketplace sellers list older models or counterfeits. Stanley is widely stocked in home centres and hardware stores, which is its main practical advantage if you need a tool today.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

