        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Tekton and Stanley are closer than their reputations suggest. Both use the right steels, both put lifetime coverage with no receipt on their mechanics tools, and both sit well below professional tool-truck prices. Tekton wins on ratchet specification, consistency across sets and the ease of its warranty claim. Stanley wins on store availability and on complete mixed kits for household jobs.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you are building a collection you will keep adding to, start with Tekton. If you need one case that covers the house and you want it today, a Stanley set is a sensible buy. Whichever you choose, compare sets by what is in the case, not by the number on the box.
          </p>
